LAYER 2 — AI QUALITY ANALYSIS
Reviewers: staff-engineer (diff-wide) + conditional specialists dispatched on domain match:
  security-engineer        (auth filter, JWT, password hashing, export audit)
  db-migration-reviewer    (V1-V7 + R__SeedAdmin)
  api-contract-reviewer    (REST controllers)
  accessibility-reviewer   (Vue views/components)
  observability-reviewer   (GlobalExceptionHandler, ETL scheduler)

═══ BLOCKERS (all fixed and re-verified) ═══════════════════════════════════════

[B1] db-migration-reviewer — Flyway disabled unconditionally; app cannot boot a real DB.
  application.yml set spring.autoconfigure.exclude for DataSourceAutoConfiguration,
  HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration and FlywayAutoConfiguration in the BASE document, and
  nothing anywhere re-enabled them. Testcontainers ITs build their own DataSource, so the
  whole suite was green while the shipping configuration would come up with no DataSource
  and never run V4/V5 or R__SeedAdmin. Fails closed, but the entire auth migration path
  had never executed in the shape it ships.
  FIX: moved the exclusion into a `spring.config.activate.on-profile: scaffold` document.
  Only IncidentDashboardApplicationTests (a context-load smoke test that deliberately wants
  no database) activates it, via @ActiveProfiles("scaffold").

[B2] staff-engineer — every Ops date filter off by one day.
  IncidentFilterBar formatted picked dates with toISOString().slice(0,10). PrimeVue's
  DatePicker emits LOCAL midnight; toISOString renders UTC. At UTC+7 picking 2 Aug sent
  2026-08-01, and the round trip back into the picker was lossy.
  Invisible in use: KPI row, heatmap, SLA buckets, trend, table and export all read the
  same filter store, so every number shifted TOGETHER and stayed mutually consistent while
  being uniformly wrong. It also wrote a window the operator never chose into
  EXPORT_AUDIT.FILTER_JSON.
  FIX: shared src/utils/date.ts (local-component formatDate + local-midnight parseDate),
  and TZ=Asia/Jakarta pinned in vitest.config.ts — under TZ=UTC the broken version PASSES,
  so the pin is what makes the test a tripwire. Verified by reverting to the broken
  implementation: 3 failures.

[B3] Change-password was a dead end that locked out the only account.
  R__SeedAdmin seeds the bootstrap admin with MUST_CHANGE_PASSWORD='Y' and the auth filter
  restricts such a session to the change-password scope — but the route rendered
  ChangePasswordPlaceholder, a component of static text with no form. A first-time admin
  could log in and then do nothing at all. Backend endpoint was complete and tested; only
  the UI was a stub, so no test caught it.
  FIX: real ChangePasswordView + 6 tests, incl. two that prove the post-change redirect
  reads auth.defaultRoute rather than a hardcoded /ops.

═══ MAJOR (fixed) ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

[M1] security-engineer — credentials recoverable from a stack trace.
  LoginRequest and ChangePasswordRequest are records, so the compiler-generated toString()
  prints every component, password included. Any framework path that formats the request
  object (bind failure, @Valid rejection, an ERROR log carrying the argument) would put a
  plaintext password in the log file.
  FIX: explicit toString() on both, masking to ***. Rule recorded in .agents/patterns/logging.md.

[M2] observability-reviewer — 4xx minted a correlation id and logged nothing.
  GlobalExceptionHandler returned the id to the caller without ever writing it. A user
  reporting "I got error abc-123" could not be matched to anything server-side.
  FIX: all 4xx paths pass the exception through respond(status, message, e), logged at WARN
  with the correlation id.

[M3] An expired access token dead-ended the SPA.
  The API client surfaced 401 to the caller with no refresh attempt, so a session that
  outlived the access-token TTL broke mid-use even though a valid refresh cookie was held.
  FIX: one silent refresh-and-retry on 401, skipped for /auth/** so a genuinely bad login
  still fails fast instead of looping.

[M4] The wallboard could not distinguish "mirror is lagging" from "service is down".
  A failed refresh left the last successful figures on screen under the stale-mirror
  banner. A responder would chase a lagging ETL when the actual fault was the dashboard
  being offline.
  FIX: refreshFailed state and a distinct message naming the real condition.

[M5] accessibility-reviewer — PrimeVue controls unlabelled to assistive tech.
  PrimeVue 4 puts `id` on the outer wrapper <div> and forwards only `input-id` to the real
  focusable control, so every <label for="..."> resolved to a div. Also: incident rows had
  no keyboard path, and the trend chart exposed no accessible data.
  FIX: input-id throughout, keyboard-activatable rows, chart data exposed to AT. Regression
  test asserts each label's `for` target is a real form control, not a wrapper.

═══ MINOR — batched to deferred-followups.md #9, not fixed here ════════════════
Naming inconsistencies, a handful of long methods in the mart-building SQL layer, and
several public methods without doc comments. None affect behaviour.

═══ NOT FIXED — recorded, needs a decision rather than code ════════════════════
[#5] Paging reconstructs an export with no audit row. /api/incidents/export is airtight,
     but a VIEWER holding only incident.ops:view can page ?size=200 and rebuild the same
     dataset with ZERO EXPORT_AUDIT rows and no rate limit. The `export` privilege is a
     bulk/convenience boundary, not a confidentiality one — worth stating explicitly
     wherever the roles are documented, because the name implies otherwise.
[#6] Redmine's is_private ACL is dropped at the mirror, so issues marked private are
     surfaced to every incident.ops:view holder. This MATCHES the source report query, so
     it may well be an accepted decision — but it is recorded nowhere, so nobody can tell
     whether it was decided or overlooked. Needs a business answer.
